      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams execute them badly, but because of what they are asked to be. A roadmap is simultaneously expected to serve as a strategy, a commitment, a communication tool, and a negotiation settlement. These purposes conflict, and the document that results usually satisfies none of them.\n\n**They confuse output with outcome.** The typical roadmap is a list of features arranged against dates. It answers *what will we build* while leaving *what will change as a result* unstated. This makes success unfalsifiable in the only way that matters: a team can ship every item on schedule and move no metric, satisfy no customer, and win no market. Because the artifact measures delivery, delivery becomes the goal, and the roadmap quietly converts a company's ambition into a shipping schedule.\n\n**They encode false confidence.** Uncertainty compounds with time. What a team knows about the next six weeks is qualitatively different from what it knows about month nine, yet most roadmaps render both in the same crisp rectangles. Once a date appears in a slide, it hardens into a promise — to sales, to executives, to customers — and the team spends the following quarters defending a forecast made when it knew the least. Learning something inconvenient becomes a political problem rather than a welcome result.\n\n**They are political settlements, not strategies.** Roadmaps often emerge from a process of appeasement: each stakeholder's priority gets a slot, and peace is purchased with inclusion. The output is a list that is technically comprehensive and strategically incoherent — no theory of why *these* bets, in *this* order, compound into advantage. Strategy requires explicit refusal, and most roadmaps contain no visible \"no.\" Where nothing has been declined, nothing has been chosen.\n\n**They ignore the physics of the organization.** Roadmaps are usually built at one hundred percent of theoretical capacity, with no allowance for maintenance, incidents, support escalations, hiring gaps, or the discovery work that precedes real building. Any of these — and all of them are certain — turns the plan into a debt schedule. The first slip cascades, and by mid-year the roadmap survives only as a document nobody consults.\n\n**They lack a feedback loop.** A plan that cannot be revised is not a plan but a prophecy. Many organizations have no ritual for killing an item, no defined evidence that would justify a change of course, and no owner empowered to make one. Sunk cost and reputational commitment keep dead initiatives alive long past their expiry.\n\nWhat survives, by contrast, looks different in kind rather than degree. It states the problems worth solving and the outcomes that would prove progress. It expresses time as confidence — now, next, later — rather than as false precision. It is short enough to be strategic and specific enough to be actionable, and it is expected to change as the team learns, which is the entire point of building anything.\n\nA roadmap should be a hypothesis about how a company will win. Most are merely an inventory of what it has promised.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams are lazy or unskilled, but because the document itself is asked to do something it cannot do: predict the future. A roadmap that lists features with delivery dates twelve months out is a forecast dressed up as a plan, and forecasts about what customers will want, what competitors will do, and how long software takes to build are wrong far more often than they are right.\n\nThe first failure is one of false certainty. Roadmaps are typically created during an annual planning cycle, when a team knows the least it will ever know about the coming year. Yet the artifact that emerges looks authoritative—neat rows, quarterly columns, confident labels. Executives read it as a promise. Sales teams sell against it. By the time the team learns that the assumptions were wrong, the roadmap has hardened into a set of obligations no one wants to renegotiate.\n\nThe second failure is the conflation of output with outcome. Most roadmaps are lists of things to ship rather than problems to solve. \"Build a mobile app\" is a feature; \"reduce time-to-first-value for new users\" is an outcome. When the roadmap specifies the solution in advance, it strips the team of the ability to discover a better one. Worse, it lets everyone declare victory when the feature launches, regardless of whether it changed anything.\n\nThe third failure is political. Roadmaps are often negotiated documents, shaped by whoever argued loudest in the planning meeting. Every stakeholder gets a line item, and prioritization dissolves into accommodation. A roadmap that tries to please everyone ends up representing no strategy at all—it becomes a to-do list with a budget attached. Real prioritization means saying no, and the roadmap is frequently where organizations avoid doing so.\n\nThe fourth failure is inertia. Good teams learn constantly: a customer interview reveals a misunderstanding, a competitor launches something unexpected, an experiment disproves a cherished belief. But the roadmap rarely absorbs this learning. Changing it feels like admitting failure, so teams keep executing against a plan they privately know is stale. The document meant to guide decisions ends up preventing them.\n\nFinally, roadmaps fail because they are disconnected from the strategy they should express. A roadmap is supposed to answer \"how do we get from here to there?\" But if \"there\" was never clearly articulated—if there is no crisp statement of which customers matter, which problems are worth solving, and what winning looks like—then the roadmap is simply a sequence of activities with no coherent reason behind it.\n\nThe alternative is not to abandon roadmaps but to change what they claim. A useful roadmap communicates intent, not commitments; problems, not solutions; sequences, not dates; and it is revised whenever the team learns something that matters. It should read less like a contract and more like a set of well-reasoned bets—bets the organization is willing to lose, and honest enough to revise.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
